{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>The Rock, His work is perfect.</b> This is clarified in the manner of what Solomon said (Ecclesiastes 7:29): ״אֲשֶׁר הָאֱלֹהִים עָשָׂה אֶת הָאָדָם יָשָׁר וְהֵמָּה בִקְשׁוּ חִשְּׁבוֹנוֹת רַבִּים״ — that the Elohim made man upright, but they sought out many devices. And this is to remove from a person's heart thoughts of iniquity, to speak falsely about HaShem — that the Craftsman who created him placed in him matter that has an inclination toward physical desires, so that this corruption came from His hand; for so we say by way of excuse: ״זְכֹר כִּי עָפָר אֲנָחְנוּ״ (Psalms 103:14), and so Job said (Job 10:9): ״זְכָר נָא כִּי כַחֹמֶר עֲשִׂיתָנִי״ etc. And perhaps the sinner will come to hang his guilt on his Maker. Concerning this it says that the matter is not so, but rather ״הַצּוּר״ — the Rock who formed man with wisdom — ״תָּמִים פׇּעֳלוֹ״, His work is perfect, for there is no painter like our Elohim (Berakhot 10a), and all His handiwork is perfect without lack. And the proof for this: ״כִּי כָל דְּרָכָיו מִשְׁפָּט״ — for all His ways are just; and had man been deficient on the side of his formation, then he would not possess free choice, for he would be coerced in his deeds and it would not be fitting to bring him into judgment for his actions. But behold, you admit that all the ways of HaShem are just, and far be it from Shaddai to do iniquity — for ״אֵל אֱמוּנָה״, a El of faithfulness, to reward the righteous, ״וְאֵין עָוֶל״, and without iniquity, to reward even the wicked. And just as you admit that in justice He gives good reward to those who do His will, so in justice He punishes the wicked — because ״צַּדִּיק וְיָשָׁר הוּא״, and He made man upright from every side, but they sought out many devices; for from their side came the corruption, not from His side, blessed be He. This is what is said: ״שִׁחֵת לוֹ״ — as its Targum renders: ״חַבִּילוּ לְהוֹן וְלָא לֵיהּ״, they corrupted themselves, not Him; for the corruption hangs on them, from the side of the evil of their choice, וְלָא לֵיהּ, not on Him, for one cannot hang the corruption on Him, blessed be He, at all. ״לֹא בָּנָיו מוּמָם״ — to say that the matter is not as they thought, to hang their blemish on what they are His children, saying: Go to the craftsman who made me, master of matter, for from His hand this came to me. Rather, the essence of their blemish is from their own side — in that they are ״דּוֹר עִקֵּשׁ וּפְתַלְתֹּל״, a twisted and perverse generation — and they have no blemish or lack whatsoever from the side that they are children of Him, blessed be He; for the Rock fashioned them in perfection without lack, but they chose for themselves corrupt ways which HaShem did not command. This also fits well according to Rashi's interpretation, who explained ״לוֹ״ as referring to the Holy One, blessed be He, meaning to say that this corruption should be attributed to Him, blessed be He — ״לֹא״, meaning this is not so; rather ״בָּנָיו מוּמָם״ — His children they were, and the corruption was their blemish, a blemish belonging to them." "Another matter: ״שִׁחֵת לוֹ״ — to attribute the corruption to Him, blessed be He; ״לֹא״ — this is not so; rather ״בָּנָיו מוּמָם״ — that the essence of their blemish is that the Holy One, blessed be He, called them His children and raised them and exalted them. Therefore it seemed plain in their eyes that HaShem would have compassion on them as a father has compassion on children, and would not look at their iniquities. And furthermore, from this their heart was lifted up to the point of corruption, and from the haughtiness of their heart they came to sin. And nearby this verse will be explained further. And see in the portion of the Stubborn and Rebellious Son for the refutation of this notion."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:15:45.927042755Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}